INT. ICE-FISHING HUT - NIGHT

A plywood box on a frozen lake. One lantern. Two stools around a hole cut in the ice, black water glinting. Lines run down into it, motionless.

ELSPETH CRANE (60s, wool cap, thermos) sits with her rod across her knees. She hasn't touched her tea.

LENNART BOYD (30s, her nephew, borrowed parka) holds his rod with both hands, jaw tight.

The ice groans somewhere out in the dark. A long, falling note.

LENNART
That's normal.

ELSPETH
Mm.

Silence. Lennart reels in an inch.

LENNART
You said nothing bites after midnight.

ELSPETH
I said nothing should.

She pours tea into the lid and sets it by his boot. He doesn't pick it up.

Down in the hole, the black water is perfectly still. Then a ring spreads outward from its center. Small, round, deliberate.

Neither of them looks at it.

LENNART
Aunt Elspeth.

ELSPETH
Hush.

Lennart's line begins to run. The reel clicks. Faster. He grips it harder; his knuckles whiten. Elspeth rests one hand on his wrist.

ELSPETH (CONT'D)
Let it go.

LENNART
It's a good one.

ELSPETH
Let it go.

He lets go. The rod slides across the ice and down the hole without a splash. The line sings out, then stops. The water goes still again.

Elspeth screws the cap on her thermos. She stands and gathers the stools. Lennart stays seated, looking at the hole, his hands open in his lap.

ELSPETH (CONT'D)
Come on.

He rises, slowly. She turns the lantern down. In the last light, the water in the hole is already skinning over with fresh ice.
